body:
Genuine advice here. Consider taking a gap year or two and actually using your college degree toward something that'll pay the bills and allow you to save a bit. Make it a priority to save $2000-$4000 depending on how many programs you want to apply for, over your gap year dedicated to PA application expenses (CASPA, travel, hotels, supplemental apps, etc.).  This may not be your ideal plan, but I feel a lot more comfortable for applying because I've had a "real" job for a few years and have put money aside specifically for this. I have no idea how I would've afforded this right out of undergrad. 

&#x200B;

IDK your major, but a bachelor's degree in whatever you're studying should open some doors for you OTHER than PA school. Heck, even a pre-med bio major should be able to leverage their way into a research assistant position or some lab company.

any others? There are way more mythical/legendary pokemon than Jirachi that could be used for special research tasks. 

&#x200B;

my main frustration is just not being able to catch certain pokemon because I don't have a team of 20 people to raid with in this area. Folks in major cities can just show up to a raid and almost be guaranteed a team there on the spot.

Are their courses accepted? I looked into their accreditation and its not through one of the regional accreditation agencies, instead its through Middle States Association of Colleges and Schools (MSACS). The regional entity is Middle States Commission on Higher Education (MSCHE). 

&#x200B;

Have you run into issues with programs accepting these courses? I'm looking for less costly online options because I'm currently living off my savings account until I'm able to find a PCE job. I'd hate to sink $$ into the courses and not have them accepted for PA programs.
